  <Document>
    <name>🌊 LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:15PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</h2><p><b>Gradient key</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/surface_currents/legend.png" width="600" alt="gradient key"><br>Surface-current speed (centimeters per second, one continuous gradient): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue stagnant &rarr; <b>10</b> teal &rarr; <b>20</b> yellow &rarr; <b>30</b> orange &rarr; <b>50</b> red &rarr; <b>75</b> red-violet &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 cm/s</b> dark-purple channel jets. Same speed always shows the same color; above 100 stays dark-purple. Needle arrows RIDE the flow — each sits on a short RK2 streamline integrated through the filed U/V field, tangent to local current (gently longer when faster: 6.5 px at 0 to 9.5 px at 50+ cm/s). Now: max <b>289</b>, median <b>15.1 cm/s</b>, median flow <b>161&deg; (S)</b>. NO DATA stays transparent — never zero.</p><p>This entry auto-refreshes from the live overlay (source: NOAA/NOS Great Lakes Operational Forecast System (GLOFS: LSOFS/LMHOFS/LEOFS/LOOFS) surface-current nowcast + NOAA/GLERL experimental GLCFS-FVCOM Huron-Erie Corridor & lower St. Marys River nowcast fill).</p><p>Turn on/off independently of all other layers. Add this file once; new source cycles appear automatically.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
